rtices in target group. The edges in input are directed (src, dst). So we need to compute total weight from each group to each other group (including possibly same group? The schema doesn't forbid self-loops? But the aggregation likely includes all edges, maybe also intra-group edges? However, the output name \"aggregate_edges\" suggests edges between groups after aggregation. But we need to check the schema: It doesn't specify that src != dst. So we should include all edges, including those from a group to itself, if there are edges within the group.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build mapping from group to list of vertices.\n- Build mapping from vertex to group (from refinement).\n- Initialize a dict or list for aggregated edges: key (src_group, dst_group) -> total weight.\n- For each edge [src, dst, weight]:\n    - src_group = refinement[src]\n    - dst_group = refinement[dst]\n    - increment total[(src_group, dst_group)] by weight\n- Then convert to list of [src_group, dst_group, total] for each unique pair. Order? Not specified. The output is an array, order not constrained. We can sort for determinism.\n- For \"members\": create dict with group->[list of vertices]. The keys are the groups from refinement.values(). Note that the schema says \"dynamicKeysFrom: case.refinement.values()\" meaning the keys must be exactly the values from refinement (the group names). So we need to include all groups, even if empty? The input refinement might have all vertices assigned, so each group appears. But if a group has no vertices? Not possible since each vertex maps to a group. But if some group value appears only in refinement? Actually the values are the groups. We need to create a key for each distinct group value. So we can extract set of values from refinement. Then for each group, list vertices that map to it. Order of vertices? Not specified, but likely sorted or as encountered. We can sort for determinism.\n\nNow, the implementation must be Python source. Use standard library only. No external libs.\n\nWe need to return a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the function source as a string.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes appropriately.\n\nWrite the function definition.\n\nLet's produce the code.\n\nImplementation details:\n- def solve(case):\n    - edges = case[\"edges\"]\n    - refinement = case[\"refinement\"]\n    - groups = set(refinement.values())\n    - members = {g: [] for g in groups}\n    - for v, g in refinement.items():\n        members[g].append(v)\n    - sort members lists? Not required but deterministic.\n    - For aggregate_edges: use defaultdict(int) from collections? But may not import? We can use dict with .get. Standard library allowed, can import collections. But we can just use plain dict.\n    - edge_totals = {}\n    - for src, dst, w in edges:\n        sg = refinement[src]\n        dg = refinement[dst]\n        key = (sg, dg)\n        edge_totals[key] = edge_totals.get(key, 0) + w\n    - aggregate_edges = [[sg, dg, total] for (sg, dg), total in edge_totals.items()]\n    - return {\"members\": members, \"aggregate_edges\": aggregate_edges}\n\nBut note: The schema requires \"members\" to have keys from refinement.values(). Our members dict has those keys. Also order of keys? Not constrained. We can keep as is.\n\nNow produce the JSON envelop with \"implementation\" key.\n\nMake sure to escape correctly.\n\nLet's write the Python code as a single string.\n\nWe'll write:\n\nimport json\n\ndef solve(case):\n    edges = case[\"edges\"]\n    refinement = case[\"refinement\"]\n    groups = set(refinement.values())\n    members = {g: [] for g in groups}\n    for v, g in refinement.items():\n        members[g].append(v)\n    # optionally sort each list for determinism\n    for g in members:\n        members[g].sort()\n    edge_totals = {}\n    for src, dst, w in edges:\n        sg = refinement[src]\n        dg = refinement[dst]\n        key = (sg, dg)\n        edge_totals[key] = edge_totals.get(key, 0) + w\n    aggregate_edges = [[sg, dg, total] for (sg, dg), total in edge_totals.items()]\n    # sort edges for determinism\n    aggregate_edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\n    return {\"members\": members, \"aggregate_edges\": aggregate_edges}\n\nBut note: The input payload includes \"_case_nonce\" field. We ignore it.\n\nNow we need to output one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being the source string.\n\nWe need to escape the source string for JSON. Use json.dumps on the source string.\n\nBut careful: The entire response must be that JSON object. So we'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"import json\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    ...\\n\"}\n\nWe need to properly escape newlines and quotes. We'll use Python to generate the string, but here we are writing the response manually. We'll construct it.\n\nWe can write the source with triple-quoted string and then escape manually? Better to use a JSON serializer, but we are producing the output manually. We'll ensure proper escaping.\n\nLet's write the source code with proper indentation and newlines as actual newlines in the string? In JSON, newlines must be escaped as \\n. So we'll replace newlines with \\n and quotes with \\\".\n\nWe'll produce the final JSON string.\n\nWe'll do it step by step.\n\nSource code:\n\nimport json\n\ndef solve(case):\n    edges = case[\"edges\"]\n    refinement = case[\"refinement\"]\n    groups = set(refinement.values())\n    members = {g: [] for g in groups}\n    for v, g in refinement.items():\n        members[g].append(v)\n    for g in members:\n        members[g].sort()\n    edge_totals = {}\n    for src, dst, w in edges:\n        sg = refinement[src]\n        dg = refinement[dst]\n        key = (sg, dg)\n        edge_totals[key] = edge_totals.get(key, 0) + w\n    aggregate_edges = [[sg, dg, total] for (sg, dg), total in edge_totals.items()]\n    aggregate_edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\n    return {\"members\": members, \"aggregate_edges\": aggregate_edges}\n\nNow escape for JSON:\n- Replace backslash with \\\\ (but there is none)\n- Replace double quote with \\\"\n- Replace newline with \\n\n- Also ensure any other control characters handled.\n\nLet's produce the escaped string.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n\"import json\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    edges = case[\\\"edges\\\"]\\n    refinement = case[\\\"refinement\\\"]\\n    groups = set(refinement.values())\\n    members = {g: [] for g in groups}\\n    for v, g in refinement.items():\\n        members[g].append(v)\\n    for g in members:\\n        members[g].sort()\\n    edge_totals = {}\\n    for src, dst, w in edges:\\n        sg = refinement[src]\\n        dg = refinement[dst]\\n        key = (sg, dg)\\n        edge_totals[key] = edge_totals.get(key, 0) + w\\n    aggregate_edges = [[sg, dg, total] for (sg, dg), total in edge_totals.items()]\\n    aggregate_edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\\n    return {\\\"members\\\": members, \\\"aggregate_edges\\\": aggregate_edges}\"\n\nNow we need to wrap with {\"implementation\": \"...\" }.\n\nMake sure the entire response is exactly that JSON object, no extra spaces? It's fine.\n\nBut note: The source code includes \"import json\" which is unused? Actually we don't use json in solve, we just return dict. So we can omit the import. The function returns a dict that will be serialized by the test harness. So we don't need to import json. Remove it.\n\nRevised source:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    edges = case[\"edges\"]\n    refinement = case[\"refinement\"]\n    groups = set(refinement.values())\n    members = {g: [] for g in groups}\n    for v, g in refinement.items():\n        members[g].append(v)\n    for g in members:\n        members[g].sort()\n    edge_totals = {}\n    for src, dst, w in edges:\n        sg = refinement[src]\n        dg = refinement[dst]\n        key = (sg, dg)\n        edge_totals[key] = edge_totals.get(key, 0) + w\n    aggregate_edges = [[sg, dg, total] for (sg, dg), total in edge_totals.items()]\n    aggregate_edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1]))\n    return {\"members\": members, \"aggregate_edges\": aggregate_edges}\n\nNow escape.\n\nWe'll produce the final JSON.\n\nAlso note: The output schema requires \"members\" to have keys exactly from refinement.values(). Our members dict has those keys, but note that if there are duplicate values in refinement, we only have one key. That's correct. Also the order of keys is not specified. So fine.\n\nNow produce the response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1326,"completion_tokens":2538,"total_tokens":3864,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1280},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2307},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1280,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":46},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}